WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
"A dream is a wish your heart makes" is from Walt Disney, and from Cinderella.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.660
That is Cinderella's Castle.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.920
It's not true, of course; a dream is a random firing of neurons that your brain is trying to interpret,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.320
but it's a lovely saying.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.580
And there are people called oneironauts

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.840
—that's the technical term they've come up with—who are dream explorers,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.420
people who do lucid dreaming.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.620
The idea that you can, sort of "wake up" in your dream,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.840
be aware that you're dreaming, and then control whatever you want:

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.560
fly, talk to people, beat fears, whatever you want to do.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.680
Now, I've tried to do this in the past; it's not been terribly successful for me,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.700
but one of the things I did learn is something that you might not know, which is how to tell if you're dreaming.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.280
There's a check you can start doing a couple of times a day,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.020
that is, apparently, quite a good trick to learning to lucid dream.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.080
And the simple test is this: read something.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.720
Read anything, the words on a sign, a newspaper, whatever you want.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:57.720
Not only will they not make sense, but they will change every time you look away and look back at them.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.820
They'll just be whatever words that your brain happens to come up with,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.780
from everything in the dictionary, utter nonsense.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.000
Whatever your brain happens to come up with, whatever neurons in the language centres of your brain happen to fire,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.840
those'll be the words you read.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.220
So, if that happens, look down at the thing in front of you right now,

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.020
I mean, I'd imagine that computers don't work in dreams, either, and that's another good sign.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.900
So either you're dreaming, or you're running Windows.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.040
But, basically, look for words. See if they change,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.280
and if they do, well... don't try and wake up,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.920
try and have a bit of fun first.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.840
That's lucid dreaming and that is something you might not have known.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.520
I think I found Donald Duck(!)

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.260
